DevOps Lab -- Jenkins Pipelines and Freestyle Projects
This file contains simple, practical steps and code for the Jenkins Pipeline and Freestyle experiments.
Before starting
Open Jenkins in your browser: http://localhost:8080.
Sign in.
For each experiment, create a separate Jenkins job using the name suggested below.
After saving, click Build Now (or Build with Parameters for the parameterized job).
Open the build number and select Console Output to see the result.
Important: Choose the correct build step
Pipeline experiments: choose New Item → Pipeline and paste the code into Pipeline → Definition → Pipeline script.
Freestyle experiments on Windows: choose Add build step → Execute Windows batch command.
Freestyle experiments on Linux: choose Add build step → Execute shell and use the Linux code supplied below.
The commands depend on the computer where the Jenkins agent runs, not just the computer where you open the browser.
Part A -- Jenkins Pipeline Experiments
Experiment 2 -- Create a simple Jenkins Pipeline
Steps
Jenkins dashboard → New Item.
Enter Simple-Pipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
Scroll to the Pipeline section.
Set Definition to Pipeline script.
Paste this code into the Script box.
Click Save → Build Now.
Open the build number → Console Output.
Pipeline code
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Hello') {
            steps {
                echo 'Hello Jenkins'
            }
        }

        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building the project'
            }
        }

        stage('Finish') {
            steps {
                echo 'Pipeline completed successfully'
            }
        }
    }
}
Expected result
The Console Output displays the three messages: Hello Jenkins, Building the project, and Pipeline completed successfully.
Experiment 3 -- Jenkins pipeline for workspace file handling
Steps
Create a new item named Workspace-File-Pipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
In the Pipeline section, choose Pipeline script.
Paste the code below.
Click Save → Build Now.
Open Console Output to check the messages and file listing.
Pipeline code -- Windows agent
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Create File') {
            steps {
                writeFile file: 'hello.txt', text: 'Hello from Jenkins workspace'
                echo 'File created'
            }
        }

        stage('Read File') {
            steps {
                script {
                    def content = readFile('hello.txt')
                    echo "File content: ${content}"
                }
            }
        }

        stage('List Files') {
            steps {
                bat 'dir'
            }
        }
    }
}
Pipeline code -- Linux agent
Use this version instead if the Jenkins agent runs Linux. The only platform-specific command here is sh 'ls'.
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Create File') {
            steps {
                writeFile file: 'hello.txt', text: 'Hello from Jenkins workspace'
                echo 'File created'
            }
        }

        stage('Read File') {
            steps {
                script {
                    def content = readFile('hello.txt')
                    echo "File content: ${content}"
                }
            }
        }

        stage('List Files') {
            steps {
                sh 'ls'
            }
        }
    }
}
Expected result
Jenkins creates hello.txt in the job workspace, reads its contents, and lists the workspace files. The Console Output should show Hello from Jenkins workspace and hello.txt.
Experiment 5 -- Parameterized Jenkins Pipeline
Steps
Create a new item named Parameterized-Pipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
In the Pipeline section, select Pipeline script.
Paste the code below.
Click Save.
Select Build with Parameters.
Enter a name and click Build.
Open Console Output.
Pipeline code
pipeline {
    agent any

    parameters {
        string(name: 'USERNAME', defaultValue: 'Student',
               description: 'Enter your name')
    }

    stages {
        stage('Welcome') {
            steps {
                echo "Welcome ${params.USERNAME}"
            }
        }
    }
}
Expected result
If you enter Ravi, the output displays Welcome Ravi. The output changes according to the name entered.
Experiment 7 -- Scheduled Jenkins Pipeline
Steps
Create a new item named Scheduled-Pipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
In the Pipeline section, select Pipeline script.
Paste the code below.
Click Save.
Jenkins will schedule the job to run approximately every five minutes.
Check Build History and open a build's Console Output.
Pipeline code
pipeline {
    agent any

    triggers {
        cron('H/5 * * * *')
    }

    stages {
        stage('Scheduled Task') {
            steps {
                echo 'Hello Jenkins'
                echo 'This is a scheduled pipeline'
            }
        }
    }
}
Expected result
Jenkins automatically starts a new build about every five minutes. H/5 means Jenkins chooses a consistent minute pattern in five-minute intervals; it may not run at exactly 00, 05, 10, etc.
Experiment 9 -- Jenkins pipeline with multiple stages
Steps
Create a new item named Multi-Stage-Pipeline.
Select Pipeline → OK.
In the Pipeline section, select Pipeline script.
Paste the code below.
Click Save → Build Now.
Open Console Output. You can also view the stage results in the Pipeline Stage View if available.
Pipeline code
pipeline {
    agent any

    stages {
        stage('Build') {
            steps {
                echo 'Building the application'
            }
        }

        stage('Test') {
            steps {
                echo 'Testing the application'
            }
        }

        stage('Deploy') {
            steps {
                echo 'Deploying the application'
            }
        }
    }
}
Expected result
The stages run in this order: Build → Test → Deploy.
Part B -- Jenkins Freestyle Experiments
Common steps for Freestyle jobs
Jenkins dashboard → New Item.
Enter the job name.
Select Freestyle project → OK.
Open the Build Steps section.
Click Add build step.
On Windows, select Execute Windows batch command. On Linux, select Execute shell.
Paste the matching code below.
Click Save → Build Now.
Open the build number → Console Output.
For Experiments 14 and 15, follow the additional configuration steps given under those experiments.
Experiment 11 -- Freestyle job to create and copy files
Windows code
Paste this into Build Steps → Execute Windows batch command.
echo Hello Jenkins > file1.txt
copy file1.txt file2.txt
echo Files created and copied successfully
dir
Linux code
Paste this into Build Steps → Execute shell.
echo "Hello Jenkins" > file1.txt
cp file1.txt file2.txt
echo "Files created and copied successfully"
ls
Expected result
The workspace contains file1.txt and file2.txt. The Console Output displays the success message and file names.
Experiment 12 -- Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) using Jenkins
This is a simple lab demonstration of the CI/CD stages. The echo commands show the stages in the Console Output; they do not perform a real deployment.
Step 1 -- Create a GitHub repository
Sign in to GitHub.
Create a repository named CICD-Demo.
Add a file named index.html with this content:
<html>
<body>
    <h1>Hello Jenkins CI/CD</h1>
</body>
</html>
Commit the file to the repository.
Copy the repository URL.
Step 2 -- Create the Jenkins Freestyle job
Jenkins dashboard → New Item.
Enter CI-CD-Demo.
Select Freestyle project → OK.
Step 3 -- Connect GitHub
In Source Code Management, select Git.
Paste the GitHub repository URL.
Set the branch to */main (use */master if your repository's default branch is master).
Step 4 -- Add the build commands
Under Build Steps, select Execute Windows batch command and paste:
echo Starting CI/CD Process
echo Building the application
echo Testing the application
echo Deploying the application
echo CI/CD completed successfully
For a Linux Jenkins agent, select Execute shell and paste:
echo "Starting CI/CD Process"
echo "Building the application"
echo "Testing the application"
echo "Deploying the application"
echo "CI/CD completed successfully"
Step 5 -- Run the job
Click Save.
Click Build Now.
Open Console Output.
Expected result
Jenkins checks out the GitHub source code and prints the Build, Test, and Deploy messages. This is a demonstration only. A real CI/CD setup needs actual build/test commands and a deployment target. To trigger builds automatically after GitHub pushes, configure a webhook or Jenkins polling trigger.
Experiment 13 -- Hello World Freestyle job and Jenkins environment variables
Windows code
echo Hello World
echo Job Name: %JOB_NAME%
echo Build Number: %BUILD_NUMBER%
echo Workspace: %WORKSPACE%
echo Jenkins Home: %JENKINS_HOME%
Linux code
echo "Hello World"
echo "Job Name: $JOB_NAME"
echo "Build Number: $BUILD_NUMBER"
echo "Workspace: $WORKSPACE"
echo "Jenkins Home: $JENKINS_HOME"
Steps
Create a Freestyle project named HelloWorld.
Go to Build Steps.
Select the correct command type for the Jenkins agent (Windows batch or Linux shell).
Paste the matching code.
Click Save → Build Now.
Open Console Output.
Expected result
The output displays Hello World and the job name, build number, workspace path, and Jenkins home path.
Meaning of the variables
JOB_NAME -- name of the Jenkins job.
BUILD_NUMBER -- current build number.
WORKSPACE -- folder used by the job.
JENKINS_HOME -- Jenkins data folder.
Experiment 14 -- Parameterized Freestyle project
Steps
Create a Freestyle project named Parameterized-Freestyle.
In General, select This project is parameterized.
Click Add Parameter → String Parameter.
Set:
Name: STUDENT_NAME
